=== layout: section

# Mendel's Law of Independent Assortment

=== layout: one-panel

# Mendel’s Four Principles of Heredity

@body

- (1) Principles of Paired Factors
  - Each organism has two alleles that make up the genotype for a given trait
- (2) Principle of Dominance
  - In each genotype there is a dominant allele, the phenotype is determined by dominant allele
- (3) Law of Segregation or Law of Purity of Gametes
  - In sexual reproduction, each parent contributes just one of their alleles to its offspring
- (4) Law of Independent Assortment
  - different pairs of alleles are passed onto the offspring independently of each other.
  - inheritance of genes at one location does not influence the inheritance of genes at another location.

=== layout: one-panel

# Mendel's 2nd Law

@body

- The Law of Independent Assortment
  - Segregation of the members of any pair of alleles is independent of the segregation of other pairs in the formation of gametes (reproductive cells).

=== layout: one-panel

# Law of Independent Assortment

@body

- Alleles at one locus segregate into gametes independently of alleles at other loci.
- [Not valid for single genes problems]{color=red}
  - not valid for blood types
  - not valid for AA x Aa
- Requires at least two genes to apply, e.g. genes A and B.
  - dihybrid (or higher) cross
    - Wrinkled/round does not affect yellow/green

=== layout: one-panel

# Hybrid Crosses

@body

- Hybrid crosses\:
  - A cross between two individuals that are both heterozygous for [one character]{color=red} is called a [monohybrid]{color=red} cross
    - [Aa × Aa]{color=red}
  - A cross between two individuals that are both heterozygous for two characters is called a dihybrid cross
    - AaBb × AaBb
  - A cross between two individuals that are both heterozygous for [three characters]{color=green} is called a [trihybrid ]{color=green}cross
    - [AaBbCc × AaBbCc]{color=green}

=== layout: one-panel


@body

![Slide image 4](assets/lect04g-indep_assort/image_004.jpg)

=== layout: section

# Dihybrid cross

=== layout: one-panel


@body

- Using a dihybrid cross, Mendel developed the law of independent assortment
  - It states that each pair of alleles segregates [independently]{color=red} of each other pair of alleles during gamete formation
  - This law applies only to [multiple genes]{color=red} on different, non-homologous chromosomes or those far apart on the same chromosome
    - Genes located near each other on the same chromosome tend to be inherited together

